00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:06.280
[♪♪]

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.680
"Annihilation" is a 2018 film written and directed by Alex Garland,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.800
based on the 2014 novel of the same name by Jeff VanderMeer.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.740
It tells the story of Lena, a woman whose relationship

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.960
with her husband, Kane, has been brought to the brink of collapse

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.400
by dissatisfaction, infidelity, absence, and trauma,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.560
and how she confronts questions of self in the face of

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.979
difficulty, depression, and the passage of time.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:31.340
It is a movie about pain and identity.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.659
The plot of the film revolves around an alien presence referred to as "the Shimmer,"

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:39.520
a mysterious field slowly absorbing swampland in the Florida Panhandle,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.400
that serves as the stage for a journey into the self

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.660
when a team of scientists, including Lena,

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.980
ventures into the Shimmer with the goal of finding its source.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.420
The question at the heart of "Annihilation" is:

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.800
How do we confront and deal with life-changing events,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.080
particularly tragedy and trauma?

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.800
Are we all fated to seek self-destruction by the coding in our very cells,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.460
to impulsively pursue our own annihilation?

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.570
Or, is it possible to come back from the brink of self-eradication?

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:12.840
VO 5: I'm a simpleton over here and I just want to talk about the aliens.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.420
DAN: So I-I'm going to --

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.080
So, I'm going to maybe end up saying some unkind things,

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.380
and I just want you to know that this really just comes

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.800
from the primal place deep inside me, the core of my being.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.060
I have nothing against any of the individuals whose work I've shown,

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.000
but the specific expressions mentioned just...hurt me.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.260
Also I - I should speak first to the fans of these videos.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.320
Hi, fans! Welcome.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.880
I don't have anything against the people who watch these kinds of videos

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.400
because I get it.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.640
You come out of a movie that impacted you -

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.260
whether it made you angry or ecstatic, it's a movie that has energized you.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.540
It has activated your ideas and your emotions and you want more of it.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.600
Maybe you need more to share in your rage and achieve symbolic closure,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.520
or you need more because it has ignited a spark

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.260
and you want to keep that spark alive.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.660
Either way, you get home

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.620
and you rush to YouTube to see what everyone else has to say about it,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.520
to share in the desired energy.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.000
I get it. I really do, because I do this too.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.560
I come out of a movie that really engages me

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.400
and immediately I want to see what everyone else has to say about it.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.620
I watch the videos my peers may have done,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.520
I read professional reviews,

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.160
I talk about it on Twitter to get people to engage,

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.060
hell, I pull up a plot summary just to keep the events fresh.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.650
I get it.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.400
But these videos are bad.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.580
VO 6: But then there's a lot of other people online who are loving this movie

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.400
and when you ask them what it's about

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.580
they just go, "Oh. I dunno."

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.540
DAN: The reason I dislike these so much

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.220
is that they are often a form of anti-intellectualism

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.860
operating on the attitude that ignorance is purity,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.000
that an understanding of culture that rejects metaphor,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.200
that rejects the symbolic and clings to the literal is more true.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.400
It is part of the process of denying art the capacity for meaning.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.000
Now, that's not to say that all of these reject metaphor in its entirety.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.760
It is rare to find someone who will entirely reject the idea of approaching film

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.479
broadly from a thematic or metaphorical point of view,

00:04:35.479 --> 00:04:38.140
but all too common to find people who will

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.600
lightly sneer at the actual attempts to do so

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.000
and suggest that it's overthinking things.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.800
And by "suggest," I mean they will say it in exactly those words.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:52.200
This is a consistent feature within modern film criticism

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.640
which, taken on the whole, is in a distinct phase

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.460
where the loudest voices in film discussion

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.520
are incurious, proudly ignorant, and approach plot

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.840
as a problem to be unpacked and solved.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.070
VO 7: Reading. [ding]

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:13.800
DAN: So why do these videos about this specific movie bother me so much?

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.080
Well, it has to do with the movie itself.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.980
If you want to talk about theme, metaphor, and meaning,

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.420
"Annihilation" is a great starting point.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:27.200
Seriously, this movie would be a fantastic film to base an entry level unit around,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.140
not because it is simple or simplistic,

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.400
but because it is dense and wears most of its themes on its sleeve.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.200
It is, in a word, blunt.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.360
Between the soundtrack and dialogue,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.700
the film straight-up tells you what it is about on multiple occasions.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.460
LENA: Okay.
